1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,162
Imagine your car breaks down on a lonely road

2
00:00:03,162 --> 00:00:06,183
in July. No shade. No signal. The air feels

3
00:00:06,183 --> 00:00:08,923
like an open oven, and the ground is so

4
00:00:08,923 --> 00:00:12,225
hot it could burn your feet through your shoes.

5
00:00:12,225 --> 00:00:16,159
Welcome to Death Valley, the hottest place ever measured

6
00:00:16,159 --> 00:00:19,180
on Earth. How long could you last out here?

7
00:00:19,180 --> 00:00:22,412
Stay to the end, because the answer depends on

8
00:00:22,412 --> 00:00:25,152
one simple thing most people get wrong.

9
00:00:25,702 --> 00:00:29,203
Welcome to Earth Decoded. If you love the most

10
00:00:29,203 --> 00:00:33,542
extreme places on our planet, tap subscribe. Today, we're

11
00:00:33,542 --> 00:00:37,499
spending one summer day in Death Valley, California.

12
00:00:38,049 --> 00:00:41,918
On July tenth, 1913, a thermometer at Furnace Creek

13
00:00:41,918 --> 00:00:46,166
in Death Valley recorded one hundred thirty four degrees

14
00:00:46,166 --> 00:00:50,642
Fahrenheit, almost fifty seven degrees Celsius. More than a

15
00:00:50,642 --> 00:00:54,739
century later, it is still the hottest air temperature

16
00:00:54,739 --> 00:00:57,697
ever officially recorded on our planet.

17
00:00:58,247 --> 00:01:01,843
And that wasn't a one-day accident. During that heat

18
00:01:01,843 --> 00:01:05,785
wave, the temperature reached at least one hundred twenty

19
00:01:05,785 --> 00:01:08,274
nine degrees for five days in a row.

20
00:01:08,824 --> 00:01:13,026
The ground gets even hotter. On July fifteenth, 1972,

21
00:01:13,026 --> 00:01:17,070
the ground at Furnace Creek reached two hundred one

22
00:01:17,070 --> 00:01:22,066
degrees Fahrenheit. That's just eleven degrees short of boiling

23
00:01:22,066 --> 00:01:22,541
water.

24
00:01:23,091 --> 00:01:26,083
So why is Death Valley so hot? First, it's

25
00:01:26,083 --> 00:01:29,573
low. Badwater Basin, in the middle of the valley,

26
00:01:29,573 --> 00:01:33,063
sits two hundred eighty two feet below sea level.

27
00:01:33,063 --> 00:01:36,339
It's the lowest point in all of North America.

28
00:01:36,889 --> 00:01:40,281
Air sinking into a low basin gets squeezed and

29
00:01:40,281 --> 00:01:43,599
heats up, like air in a bicycle pump. Second,

30
00:01:43,599 --> 00:01:47,212
the valley is long, narrow and surrounded by tall

31
00:01:47,212 --> 00:01:50,899
mountains, so the hot air gets trapped and circles

32
00:01:50,899 --> 00:01:52,889
around instead of escaping.

33
00:01:53,439 --> 00:01:57,232
Third, almost nothing grows on the valley floor and

34
00:01:57,232 --> 00:02:00,504
the air is very dry. Sunlight hits bare rock

35
00:02:00,504 --> 00:02:03,850
and salt, the ground heats up, and the ground

36
00:02:03,850 --> 00:02:06,602
heats the air above it, all day long.

37
00:02:07,152 --> 00:02:10,755
And here's the scary part. In summer, nights barely

38
00:02:10,755 --> 00:02:14,711
cool down. Some nights the temperature never drops below

39
00:02:14,711 --> 00:02:18,525
one hundred degrees Fahrenheit. Your body never gets a

40
00:02:18,525 --> 00:02:18,949
break.

41
00:02:19,499 --> 00:02:22,709
Quick question before we go on. What's the hottest

42
00:02:22,709 --> 00:02:26,625
temperature you've ever experienced? Tell me in the comments.

43
00:02:27,175 --> 00:02:30,066
Back to you, stranded on the road. In the

44
00:02:30,066 --> 00:02:32,958
first hour, you sweat. A lot. In this dry

45
00:02:32,958 --> 00:02:36,555
air, sweat evaporates the moment it appears, so you

46
00:02:36,555 --> 00:02:39,799
might not even feel wet. That's a trap. You're

47
00:02:39,799 --> 00:02:42,620
losing water much faster than you think.

48
00:02:43,170 --> 00:02:46,452
In extreme heat, a person can lose more than

49
00:02:46,452 --> 00:02:49,808
a liter of sweat every hour. Without water to

50
00:02:49,808 --> 00:02:53,239
replace it, you start getting dehydrated fast.

51
00:02:53,789 --> 00:02:56,821
By hour three, you feel dizzy and your head

52
00:02:56,821 --> 00:03:00,417
pounds. Your mouth is dry, your heart beats faster,

53
00:03:00,417 --> 00:03:04,084
and your muscles cramp. This is heat exhaustion, and

54
00:03:04,084 --> 00:03:06,269
it's your body's warning siren.

55
00:03:06,819 --> 00:03:09,748
If you keep walking in the sun, things get

56
00:03:09,748 --> 00:03:13,375
dangerous. When your body can no longer cool itself,

57
00:03:13,375 --> 00:03:17,419
your core temperature climbs past one hundred four degrees

58
00:03:17,419 --> 00:03:21,603
Fahrenheit. That's heatstroke, and it's a medical emergency.

59
00:03:22,153 --> 00:03:26,144
With heatstroke, people get confused, stop sweating and can

60
00:03:26,144 --> 00:03:29,999
collapse. Rangers say that's how many heat tragedies here

61
00:03:29,999 --> 00:03:32,840
happen: people try to walk for help in the

62
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:34,057
middle of the day.

63
00:03:34,607 --> 00:03:38,186
So here's that simple thing most people get wrong.

64
00:03:38,186 --> 00:03:41,406
If your car breaks down in Death Valley, stay

65
00:03:41,406 --> 00:03:44,556
with the car. It gives shade, it's easier to

66
00:03:44,556 --> 00:03:48,349
spot, and rangers patrol the roads. Drink your water,

67
00:03:48,349 --> 00:03:51,355
don't ration it, and only move in the cool

68
00:03:51,355 --> 00:03:52,357
early morning.

69
00:03:52,907 --> 00:03:55,737
The park says to drink at least a gallon

70
00:03:55,737 --> 00:03:58,851
of water a day in summer, about four liters,

71
00:03:58,851 --> 00:04:02,389
and even more if you're active. That's why rangers

72
00:04:02,389 --> 00:04:05,856
say: travel prepared, and stay on the main roads.

73
00:04:06,406 --> 00:04:09,256
Be honest. Would you have stayed with the car,

74
00:04:09,256 --> 00:04:12,230
or started walking? Let me know in the comments.

75
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:16,098
So how did Death Valley get its name? In

76
00:04:16,098 --> 00:04:20,163
1849, during the California Gold Rush, a group of

77
00:04:20,163 --> 00:04:24,642
pioneers took what they thought was a shortcut through

78
00:04:24,642 --> 00:04:27,628
the valley. They got lost for weeks.

79
00:04:28,178 --> 00:04:31,925
When they finally climbed out over the mountains, legend

80
00:04:31,925 --> 00:04:35,070
says one of them turned back and said, Goodbye,

81
00:04:35,070 --> 00:04:37,010
Death Valley. The name stuck.

82
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:41,671
Later, miners came for gold, silver and borax. Teams

83
00:04:41,671 --> 00:04:45,623
of twenty mules pulled huge wagons of borax across

84
00:04:45,623 --> 00:04:49,418
the desert, more than one hundred sixty miles to

85
00:04:49,418 --> 00:04:50,445
the railroad.

86
00:04:50,995 --> 00:04:54,875
Long before the pioneers, people already called this valley

87
00:04:54,875 --> 00:04:58,294
home. The Timbisha Shoshone have lived here for more

88
00:04:58,294 --> 00:05:01,319
than a thousand years. They knew where to find

89
00:05:01,319 --> 00:05:04,344
springs, which plants to eat, and when to move

90
00:05:04,344 --> 00:05:06,974
up into the cooler mountains for summer.

91
00:05:07,524 --> 00:05:10,902
Their name for the valley is Tümpisa, which means

92
00:05:10,902 --> 00:05:14,142
rock paint, after the red ochre clay they used.

93
00:05:14,142 --> 00:05:18,209
Today their village still stands near Furnace Creek, inside

94
00:05:18,209 --> 00:05:19,449
the national park.

95
00:05:19,999 --> 00:05:23,505
You'd think nothing could live here. But Death Valley

96
00:05:23,505 --> 00:05:26,812
is full of survivors. Kangaroo rats can live their

97
00:05:26,812 --> 00:05:30,847
whole lives without drinking water, getting moisture from the

98
00:05:30,847 --> 00:05:31,839
seeds they eat.

99
00:05:32,389 --> 00:05:35,749
In tiny pools of water that survived from the

100
00:05:35,749 --> 00:05:39,184
last ice age live the Devils Hole pupfish, one

101
00:05:39,184 --> 00:05:42,245
of the rarest fish on Earth. They live in

102
00:05:42,245 --> 00:05:44,635
one small pool and nowhere else.

103
00:05:45,185 --> 00:05:49,311
And after a rare wet winter, something magical happens.

104
00:05:49,311 --> 00:05:53,512
The desert explodes into a superbloom, carpets of yellow

105
00:05:53,512 --> 00:05:56,363
wildflowers covering the valley floor.

106
00:05:56,913 --> 00:06:01,044
Death Valley also has one of nature's great mysteries:

107
00:06:01,044 --> 00:06:05,327
the sailing stones of Racetrack Playa. Heavy rocks leave

108
00:06:05,327 --> 00:06:08,845
long trails across the dry lakebed, as if they

109
00:06:08,845 --> 00:06:10,375
moved by themselves.

110
00:06:10,925 --> 00:06:14,869
For decades nobody could explain it. Then, in 2014,

111
00:06:14,869 --> 00:06:19,354
scientists filmed it. On rare winter mornings, thin sheets

112
00:06:19,354 --> 00:06:22,834
of ice form on shallow water, and light winds

113
00:06:22,834 --> 00:06:26,391
push the ice, carrying the rocks with it, inch

114
00:06:26,391 --> 00:06:27,010
by inch.

115
00:06:27,560 --> 00:06:31,366
There's even snow here. About eighty five miles from

116
00:06:31,366 --> 00:06:35,245
Badwater sits Mount Whitney, the highest point in the

117
00:06:35,245 --> 00:06:39,270
lower forty eight states. The lowest and highest points

118
00:06:39,270 --> 00:06:42,344
of the contiguous US, almost side by side.

119
00:06:42,894 --> 00:06:46,767
Here's something hard to imagine. During the last ice

120
00:06:46,767 --> 00:06:50,202
age, Death Valley was filled with water. A huge

121
00:06:50,202 --> 00:06:54,294
lake, Lake Manly, stretched about one hundred miles long

122
00:06:54,294 --> 00:06:56,633
and up to six hundred feet deep.

123
00:06:57,183 --> 00:07:01,343
When the climate warmed, the lake evaporated, leaving behind

124
00:07:01,343 --> 00:07:04,671
the salt flats of Badwater. That white crust you

125
00:07:04,671 --> 00:07:07,721
see today is what's left of an ancient lake.

126
00:07:08,271 --> 00:07:12,602
And Death Valley has volcanoes too. Ubehebe Crater, about

127
00:07:12,602 --> 00:07:16,022
half a mile wide, was blasted out when rising

128
00:07:16,022 --> 00:07:20,049
magma hit groundwater and exploded into steam, just a

129
00:07:20,049 --> 00:07:21,797
few thousand years ago.

130
00:07:22,347 --> 00:07:26,736
Death Valley keeps breaking records. In 2020 and again

131
00:07:26,736 --> 00:07:31,126
in 2021, it hit one hundred thirty degrees Fahrenheit,

132
00:07:31,126 --> 00:07:36,003
some of the hottest temperatures reliably measured in modern

133
00:07:36,003 --> 00:07:36,491
times.

134
00:07:37,041 --> 00:07:40,299
Yet more than a million people visit every year,

135
00:07:40,299 --> 00:07:43,014
many of them in summer, just to feel the

136
00:07:43,014 --> 00:07:46,340
heat and take a photo with the famous thermometer

137
00:07:46,340 --> 00:07:47,494
at Furnace Creek.

138
00:07:48,044 --> 00:07:51,463
Some people come here in July on purpose. Every

139
00:07:51,463 --> 00:07:55,318
summer, runners take on the Badwater one thirty five,

140
00:07:55,318 --> 00:07:58,592
a race of one hundred thirty five miles, from

141
00:07:58,592 --> 00:08:01,719
Badwater Basin all the way up to the slopes

142
00:08:01,719 --> 00:08:02,956
of Mount Whitney.

143
00:08:03,506 --> 00:08:07,016
Runners have support crews who spray them with water

144
00:08:07,016 --> 00:08:09,852
and bring ice, and many run at night. It's

145
00:08:09,852 --> 00:08:12,957
often called one of the toughest foot races on

146
00:08:12,957 --> 00:08:13,362
Earth.

147
00:08:13,912 --> 00:08:17,236
And when the sun goes down, Death Valley shows

148
00:08:17,236 --> 00:08:21,137
another side. It's an International Dark Sky Park, one

149
00:08:21,137 --> 00:08:23,666
of the best places in the US to see

150
00:08:23,666 --> 00:08:26,051
the Milky Way with your own eyes.

151
00:08:26,601 --> 00:08:29,738
So, could you survive a summer day in Death

152
00:08:29,738 --> 00:08:33,971
Valley? With water, shade and patience, yes. Without them,

153
00:08:33,971 --> 00:08:37,181
in the middle of a July afternoon, your body

154
00:08:37,181 --> 00:08:40,392
could be in serious danger within just a few

155
00:08:40,392 --> 00:08:40,830
hours.

156
00:08:41,380 --> 00:08:45,280
Death Valley isn't evil. It's just extreme. Respect it,

157
00:08:45,280 --> 00:08:48,400
and it's one of the most beautiful places on

158
00:08:48,400 --> 00:08:48,825
Earth.

159
00:08:49,375 --> 00:08:53,077
Last question. Where should we go next? Somewhere just

160
00:08:53,077 --> 00:08:56,161
as extreme, but freezing cold? Tell me in the

161
00:08:56,161 --> 00:08:56,778
comments.

162
00:08:57,328 --> 00:09:01,037
Thanks for watching Earth Decoded. If you enjoyed this,

163
00:09:01,037 --> 00:09:04,140
like the video, subscribe, and share it with a

164
00:09:04,140 --> 00:09:07,310
friend who complains about the heat. See you in

165
00:09:07,310 --> 00:09:08,187
the next one.

